A '''Fenwick tree''' or '''binary indexed tree''' '''(BIT)''' is a data structure that can efficiently update elements and calculate [[prefix sum]]s in a table of numbers.

This structure was proposed by Boris Ryabko in 1989<ref>{{cite journal |author=Boris Ryabko |title=A fast on-line code |journal=Soviet Math. Dokl. |volume=39 |issue=3 |year=1989 |pages=533–537 |url=http://boris.ryabko.net/dan1989.pdf }}</ref>
with a further modification published in 1992.<ref>{{cite journal |author=Boris Ryabko |title=A fast on-line adaptive code |journal=IEEE Transactions on Information Theory |volume=28 |issue=1 |year=1992 |pages=1400–1404 |url=http://boris.ryabko.net/ryabko1992.pdf}}</ref>
It has subsequently become known under the name Fenwick tree after [[Peter Fenwick (computer scientist)|Peter Fenwick]], who described this structure in his 1994 article.<ref name=":0">{{cite journal |author=Peter M. Fenwick |title=A new data structure for cumulative frequency tables |journal=Software: Practice and Experience |volume=24 |issue=3 |year=1994 |pages=327–336 |doi=10.1002/spe.4380240306 |citeseerx=10.1.1.14.8917 |s2cid=7519761 }}</ref>

When compared with a flat array of numbers, the Fenwick tree achieves a much better balance between two operations: element update and prefix sum calculation. A flat array of <math>n</math> numbers can either store the elements or the prefix sums. In the first case, computing prefix sums requires linear time; in the second case, updating the array elements requires linear time (in both cases, the other operation can be performed in constant time). Fenwick trees allow both operations to be performed in <math>O(\log n)</math> time. This is achieved by representing the numbers as a [[Tree (data structure)|tree]] with <math>n+1</math> nodes where the value of each node in the tree is the prefix sum of the array from the index of the parent (inclusive) up to the index of the node (exclusive). The tree itself is implicit and can be stored as an array of <math>n</math> numbers, with the implicit root node omitted from the array. The tree structure allows the operations of element retrieval, element update, prefix sum, and range sum to be performed using only <math>O(\log n)</math> node accesses.

==Motivation==
{{main|Prefix sums#Applications}}

Given a table of elements, it is sometimes desirable to calculate the [[running total]] of values up to each index according to some [[Associative property|associative]] [[binary operation]] (addition on integers being by far the most common). Fenwick trees provide a method to query the running total at any index, in addition to allowing changes to the underlying value table and having all further queries reflect those changes.

Fenwick trees are particularly designed to implement the [[arithmetic coding]] algorithm, which maintains counts of each symbol produced and needs to convert those to the [[cumulative probability]] of a symbol less than a given symbol.  Development of operations it supports were primarily motivated by use in that case.

Using a Fenwick tree it requires only <math>O(\log n)</math> operations to compute any desired cumulative sum, or more generally the sum of any range of values (not necessarily starting at zero). It is also possible to construct extensions of this data structure to quickly compute cumulative sums on <math>d</math>-dimensional arrays in <math>O(\log^d n)</math> time.<ref name=":1">{{cite journal |author=Pushkar Mishra |year=2013 |title=A New Algorithm for Updating and Querying Sub-arrays of Multidimensional Arrays |arxiv=1311.6093 |doi=10.13140/RG.2.1.2394.2485 |s2cid=1561192}}</ref>


==Description==
A Fenwick tree is most easily understood by considering a [[one-based indexing|one-based array]] <math>A[n]</math> with <math>n</math> elements. The corresponding Fenwick tree has <math>n+1</math> nodes with an implicit node 0 at the root. Each level <math>k</math> of the tree contains nodes with indices corresponding to sums of <math>k</math> distinct powers of 2 (with <math>k=0</math> representing an empty sum 0). For example, level <math>k=1</math> contains nodes <math>1=2^0, 2=2^1, 4=2^2, ...</math> and level <math>k=2</math> contains nodes <math>3=2^1 + 2^0, 5 = 2^2 + 2^0, 6 = 2^2 + 2^1, ...</math> The parent of a given node can be found by clearing the [[Find_first_set|last set bit]] (LSB) in its index, corresponding to the smallest power of 2 in its sum. For example, the parent of 6 = 110<sub>2</sub> is 4 = 100<sub>2</sub> .

The below diagram shows the structure of a 16-node Fenwick tree, corresponding to a 15-element array A:
[[File:16-node Fenwick tree.svg|center|thumb|438x438px|Depiction of a 16-node Fenwick tree containing range sums of a 15-node array A]]

Let <math>A(i,j] = \{ A[k] \}_{k=i+1}^j</math>. The value of a node at index <math>i</math> corresponds to the [[Range query (data structures)|range sum]] of elements in <math>A(\mathrm{parent}(i),i]</math>, that is, the values of A starting after the parent's index up to the current node's index, inclusive. The elements <math>A(\mathrm{parent}(i),i]</math> are considered to be the "range of responsibility" for the current node<ref name=":0" /> and consist of <math>\mathrm{lsb}(i) = (i\ \&\ (-i))</math> (where & denotes bitwise AND) elements. Note that the indices in this range do '''not''' directly correspond to children of <math>i</math>: for example, the range of responsibility for node 2 is <math>A(0,2] = \{A[1], A[2]\}</math> but node 1 is not a child of node 2. The root node 0 contains the sum of the empty range <math>A(0,0] = \{\}</math> with value 0.

Typically, the Fenwick tree is implemented as an [[implicit data structure]] using a flat array analogous to implementations of a [[binary heap]]. In this representation, the root node 0 is omitted and the array indices directly correspond to node indices in the tree (using 1-based indexing).

The initial process of building the Fenwick tree over a table of values runs in <math>O(n log n)</math> time. Other efficient operations include locating the index of a value if all values are positive, or all indices with a given value if all values are non-negative. Also supported is the scaling of all values by a constant factor in <math>O(n)</math> time.

=== Max/min range query ===

By keeping track of min/max in following intervals, where index = k2^i (k is a odd number), one can find max/min in [l,r] range :
<ref>{{cite web |title=Efficient Range Minimum Queries using Binary Indexed Trees|url=https://ioinformatics.org/journal/v9_2015_39_44.pdf}}</ref>

BIT1[i] = [(k-1)2^i+1,k2^i]

BIT2[i] =  [k2^i,(k+1)2^i-1]

This means:
* One look at all nodes from first node in subtree to this node, to this node.
* One look at all of the nodes, to the right of ones position, of this subtree one are part of - except the root node.

By remembering smallest to the left and smallest to the right of a spesific index, in the same subtree, one can jump between different min/max based on index.

A special case is where the node k2^i, that is part of both BIT1 and BIT2, is max/min.

The normal [0,r] max/min query can be implemented in same way as sum/product.

=== Range sum query ===
To find the prefix sum up to any given index (the ''"range query"'' operation), add up the values of nodes along the path from the current index to the root of the tree (which is trivially an empty sum, 0). The number of values to add (excluding the implicit root) is equal to the number of 1-bits in the index, and is at most <math>\lceil\log_2 n\rceil</math>, giving a time complexity of <math>O(\log n)</math>.

For example, say one wishes to find the sum of the first eleven values. Eleven is 1011<sub>2</sub> in binary. This contains three 1-bits, so three node values must be added: those at nodes 11=1011<sub>2</sub>, 10=1010<sub>2</sub>, and 8=1000<sub>2</sub> (the implicit root node of 0000<sub>2</sub> can be ignored). These contain the sums of A[11], A[9,10], and A[1,8], respectively (where A[i,j] = {A[i], A[i+1], ..., A[j]}) .

=== Point update ===
To update the value in a Fenwick tree at index <math>i</math> (corresponding to assigning <math>A[i]</math> a new value) (the ''"point update"'' operation):

* Compute the delta <math display="inline">\delta = A[i]_\mathrm{new} - A[i]_\mathrm{old}</math>.
* Then, while index <math>i \leq n</math>:
** Update the index by adding LSB: <math>i \leftarrow i + (i\ \&\ (-i))</math>
** Add <math>\delta</math> to the value at node <math>i</math>.

Intuitively, this can be thought of as updating each node (starting with <math>i</math> and iterating in increasing order) whose range of responsibility includes <math>A[i]</math>. 

For example, updating value 11=1011<sub>2</sub> in a 16-element array requires updating 1011<sub>2</sub>, (1011<sub>2</sub> + 1<sub>2</sub> = 1100<sub>2</sub>), and (1100<sub>2</sub> + 100<sub>2</sub> = 10000<sub>2</sub>).  These contain the sums of A[11], A[9,12], and A[1,16], respectively. Again, the maximum number of nodes that need to be updated is limited by the number of bits in the size of the array, <math>\lceil\log_2 n\rceil</math>, and the time complexity is thus <math>O(\log n)</math>.

=== Building the tree ===
A naive way to construct the tree would be to initialize the tree values to 0 and perform <math>n</math> point updates, yielding a time complexity of <math>O(n\log n)</math>. However, an alternate approach uses [[dynamic programming]] to build the tree up incrementally along increasing indices. Initialization proceeds as follows:

* Copy array <math>A[n]</math> into array <math>T[n]</math> containing tree values
* For each index ''i'' from 1 to n:
** Let <math>j = i + (i\ \& \ (-i))</math>. This is the first node larger than <math>i</math> that contains <math>A[i]</math> in its range of responsibility.
** If <math>j  \leq n</math>, update value of node at j: <math>T[j] \leftarrow T[j] + T[i]</math>

It can be shown that at the point that index <math>i</math> is reached in the loop, <math>T[i]</math> is already the correct Fenwick value for node <math>i</math>. If <math>i</math> is a leaf node (with only one element in its range of responsibility) this holds trivially. If <math>i</math> is an internal node, the loop ensures that it has been updated with all range sums within its own range, so it will contain the correct Fenwick value. For example, at step <math>i=4</math>, node 4 (with initial value <math>A[4]</math>) will have been incremented by <math>T[2]</math> (which contains the sum <math>A[1] + A[2]</math>) and <math>T[3]</math> (which contains the value <math>A[3]</math>), and thus will contain the range sum <math>A(0,4]</math> of the first 4 elements of the tree.

Since each update happens at most once per index, the resulting algorithm is <math>O(n)</math> time complexity. This can also be done in-place in the original array, eliminating the copy and additional storage for <math>T[n]</math>.

An analogous operation can be performed to convert a Fenwick tree back into the original frequency array <math>A[n]</math>, by iterating backwards from n to 1 and subtracting the value of <math>j = i + (i\ \& \ (-i))</math> from the value of <math>i</math> at each timestep.

A less efficient <math>O(n)</math> algorithm for constructing the tree works in two passes: first convert <math>A[n]</math> to a prefix sum (which takes <math>O(n)</math> time), then iterating backwards from n..1, compute each node's range sum by computing the difference of prefix sums: <math>\Sigma A(\mathrm{parent}(i), i] = \Sigma A(0, i] - \Sigma A(0, \mathrm{parent}(i)]</math>.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Fenwick tree: initialization in O(N) |url=https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/59305 |access-date=2023-01-23 |website=Codeforces |language=en}}</ref>

=== Extension to multiple dimensions ===
A ''2D Fenwick tree (2D BIT)'' can be constructed as a 1D Fenwick tree where each node of the tree contains a 1D Fenwick tree,<ref name=":1" /> and stores range sums for submatrices of a 2D matrix ''A''[m,n]. The tree representation remains implicit and the data is stored in an ''m'' x ''n'' array where position (i, j) in the array corresponds to the jth node of the ith Fenwick tree.

Point update and range query are implemented similarly to the 1D case, but using nested loops (iterating along the column dimension (sub-tree) in the inner loop, and iterating along the row dimension (main tree) in the outer loop).

This idea can be extended to tensors with arbitrary number of dimensions ''d,'' with time complexity <math>O(\log^d n)</math> for range-query and point-update (assuming the tensor is size ''n'' along each axis).

=== Fenwick tree as a compression of a binary tree ===

A binary tree can be compressed to a fenwick tree, if and only if, for every even leaf node in binary tree (indexed 2n, n is integer ),
it is has the same property as its parents, up to the point; the smallest sub-tree that is possible to make,connecting as many nodes as possible, is created

To decompress, just do the opposit as done in compression

Subtrees may also be compressed to fenwick tree.

Furthermore, if only small changes is needed to make an fenwick compression, one should remember and do necessery changes - with the changes as extra information, to enable decompression.

==== Generate fenwick compressable binary tree from its leafs ====
A fenwick compressable binary tree can be generated from its leaf nodes, if all leaf nodes with index k2^i is connected to node with index 2^i(k+1)  
in such a way node with index 2^i(k+1)  is parent of node with index k2^i. To find 2^i term in val = k2^i, use val & (-val). Parent node is then: (val & (-val)) + k2^i

Example:

Node 14 is connected to node 16, because 14 = 7*2 and 16 = 7*2 +2.
4 is connected to 8, because 4 = 2^2  and 8 = 2^2 + 2^2
12 is connected to 16 because, 12 = 3*(2^2) and 16 = 3*(2^2) + 2^2

==== Finding i and k ====

Recall 2^i = (val & (-val)), where val =  k2^i.

By simple algebraic manipulation, one see that:

k = val / (val & (-val))

By using ln, and some simple algebraic manipulation, one see that:

i = ln (val / k) / ln(2), where val = k2^i.

===== Multiple nodes that has same parent =====

How to handle cases where a several nodes have this node as parent?

Because first parent can have 1 child, second parent can have 2 childs, third parent can have 3 childs,counted from index 1  
.One place a node in a index, such that there are enough childs. If one have to many childs, one can make them dummy nodes - that just takes up space.

A parent childs, counted from index 1 with N childs, have childs that can have N-1,N-2,...2,1,0 (or N-1,N-2,...2,1 if it is a root node)  childs where closest child only can have 1 or 0 childs, depending on whether it is a root node or not. Furthermore; longest sequence length from leaf node to this node, is equal to number of possible childs for this node.

As a consequence; it is only possible to add a tree to a node in fenwick tree, if: 

* If longest sequence is length N, the node that adds the tree must have the possibility to add at least N childs.
* For each node in subtree, all direct childs of same node must have a unique number of childs. That is; no two direct childs of same node, can have same number of childs.
* Every sequence from node in subtree to leaf, must have a strictly descending sequence of number of childs

===== Node that has no parent =====

To handle the case where a node has no parent, one can place a dummy node as a parent.
Such a dummy node could be a node that can be identfied in a node, such that one reset the value to 0 or 1, when doing prefix sum and prefix product - it kinda forget the sum and product so far.

==Implementation==
=== Basic implementation in [[C (programming language)|C]] ===
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">
// SIZE should be 1 + a power of 2.
int A[SIZE];

// Least Significant Bit of i having a value of 1
#define LSB(i) ((i) & -(i))

// Returns the sum of the first i elements (indices 0 to i)
// Equivalent to range_sum(0, i)
int prefix_sum(int i) {
	int sum = A[0];
	for (; i != 0; i -= LSB(i))
		sum += A[i];
	return sum;
}

// Add delta to element with index i (zero-based)
void add(int i, int delta) {
	if (i == 0) {
		A[0] += delta;
		return;
	}
	for (; i < SIZE; i+= LSB(i))
		A[i] += delta;
}
</syntaxhighlight>

=== Useful functions in [[C (programming language)|C]] ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="c">
// Returns the sum of elements from i + 1 to j
// Equivalent to prefix_sum(j) - prefix_sum(i), but slightly faster
int range_sum(int i, int j) {
	int sum = 0;
	for (; j > i; j -= LSB(j))
		sum += A[j];
	for (; i > j; i -= LSB(i))
		sum -= A[i];
	return sum;
}

// Convert A[] in place to Fenwick tree form
void init(void) {
	for (int i = 1; i < SIZE; ++i) {
		int j = i + LSB(i);
		if (j < SIZE)
			A[j] += A[i];
	}
}

// Convert back to array of per-element counts
void fini(void) {
	for (int i = SIZE - 1; i > 0; --i) {
		int j = i + LSB(i);
		if (j < SIZE)
			A[j] -= A[i];
	}
}

// Return a single element's value
int get(int i) {
	return range_sum(i, i + 1);
}

// Set (as opposed to adjust) a single element's value
void set(int i, int value) {
	add(i, value - get(i));
}

// Find the largest i with prefix_sum(i) <= value.
// NOTE: Requires that all values are non-negative!
unsigned int rank_query(int value) {
	int i = 0, j = SIZE - 1;
	// j is a power of 2.

    value -= A[0];
	for (; j > 0;  j >>= 1) {
		if (i + j < SIZE && A[i + j] <= value) {
			value -= A[i + j];
			i += j;
		}
	}
	return i;
}
</syntaxhighlight>

=== Implementation in [[C++]] ===
<syntaxhighlight lang="c++">
class FenwickTree {
private:
    vector<int> data;

    int getParent(int i) const {
        return i - (i & (-i));
    }

    int getNext(int i) const {
        return i + (i & (-i));
    }

public:
    FenwickTree(int n) : data(n+1, 0) {
    }

    int getSum(int i) const {
        int sum = 0;
        ++i;
        while (i > 0) {
            sum += data[i];
            i = getParent(i);
        }
        return sum;
    }

    void update(int i, int v) {
        ++i;
        while (i < data.size()) {
            data[i] += v;
            i = getNext(i);
        }
    }
};
</syntaxhighlight>
==Applications==


===Keeping track of positions problem===

Assume one have a set of elements, with an integer displacement. Then Array[i] would store relative displacement to previous element, where i is an element number.
By taking range sum between i and j, one would get the relative distance between object i and j.

A concrete real example of this would be tags in html file, where one keep track of line numbers of every element when an element is moved.
To get a position, one simply take the range sum between two elements.

=== Finding angle that makes a ray hit maximum number of mirrors, using bounding primitives ===

Let fenwick tree be a tree representing number of mirrors that is hit by incoming angles, for a ray from a spesific point, against 2D bounding primives (circle and squares) surounding smaller bounding primitves, a group of mirrors or a mirror.
A mirror can have different ranges for incoming rays on left side, and incoming rays on right side.

If one use k-means multiple times, one can create a tree. Who is parent and who is child must be placed into fenwick tree.This is done such that an update of accepted incoming range, of a mirror, gets updated to its bouding primitvie, and further upward in the tree - bounding primitives sums accepted ranges for a mirror to other accepted ranges for mirrors.

By using range search, one can find angles of ray, from a spesific point, that hits  maximum number of mirrors - that in some cases could be all mirrors.

=== Sub-range product of transformation matrices===

Create two fenwick trees, one with product over inverses and one with product of its usal matrices.

To find sub-range product, take product range of matrices in inverse fenwick tree to left side of range.
Remember this matrix.

Now, take product range of matrices in fenwick tree to right side of range.

Take product of this matrix with the inverse matrix one got from inverse fenwick tree.

By doing this, one can update a matrix, without doing all the recalculations. Important thing here, is to update both matrix and its inverse

=== Longest increasing subsequence ===
Consider a sequence

8 5 7 3 1 2 9 4 6

For each element v added, update A[i] s.t:

A[i] = max(0 <= x < i) A[x] + 1, such that array[x] <= array[i].

array[x] <= array[i] can be ensured, by letting i= array[index in list]. 

This leaves ous with:

A[i] = max(0 <= x < i) A[x] + 1,

A running example:

#A[array[0]] = A[8] = max (0 <= x < 8) A[x] + 1 = max(0,0,0,...,0) + 1 = 1
#A[array[1]] = A[5] = max (0 <= x < 5) A[x] + 1 = max(0,0,0,0,...,0) + 1 = 1
#A[array[2]] = A[7] = max (0 <= x < 7) A[x]+ 1 = max(A[0],[1],A[2],A[3],A[4],A[5],A[6]) + 1 = A[5] + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2
#A[array[3]] = A[3] = max (0 <= x < 3) A[x] + 1 = max(0,0,0...,0) + 1 = 1
#A[array[4]] = A[1] = max (0 <= x < 1) A[x] + 1 = max(A[0]) + 1 = 1
#A[array[5]] = A[2] = max (0 <= x < 2) A[x] + 1 = max(0,1) + 1 = 2
#A[array[6]] = A[9] = max (0 <= x < 9) A[x] + 1 = max(0,1,2,1,0,1,0,2,1) + 1 = 2+1 = 3
#A[array[7]] = A[4] = max (0 <= x < 4) A[x] + 1 = max(0,1,2,1) + 1 = 2+1 = 3
#A[array[8]] = A[6] = max (0 <= x < 6) A[x] + 1 = max(0,1,2,1,3,1) + 1 = 3+1 = 4

That corresponds well to the answer 1,2,4,6

If max element is to large, one can use number compression by using sorting

That is i= indexInSortedList[index in not sorted list], instead of i = array[index in not sorted list]

Take this example:

* Array: 166,32,44,66,88
* Sorted: 32,44,66,88,166

Then:

A[indexInSortedList[0]] = A[4], and we are back to the problem we are used to

=== Find range with smallest sum that contains all elements in set S ===

Main idea:

Remember last index of each element in the sequence array, that is found in set S.
One has a range where both left and right side can be moved. Because only last occurrence is remembered,
removing elements from left side won't do any damage. 

One can check if an element is already found, by checking it against an array of indexes of last occurrence for this number. Furthermore, one can check if index one is standing on, is last occurrence by comparing with index in array.

<syntaxhighlight lang="c++">
for all elements in array:


if array[i] is not in S:

#Element is new
elif A[array[i]] is 0:
  A[array[i]] = i
  elementFound = elementFound + 1

else:
  #update index of last occurance of this number
  A[array[i]] = i


if elementsFound = nmb elements in S:

   rangeSum = calculate range sum for range
   if rangeSum < smallestRangeSum:
     smallestRangeSum = rangeSum
     bestLeftIdx = left index
     bestRightIdx = i

   if A[array[left index]] = left index:
      elementsFound = elementFound - 1
      A[array[left index]] = 0

   left index = left index +1


   while A[array[left index]] not left index or array[left index] not in S:
     left index = left index + 1
i=i+1

</syntaxhighlight>


If max element is to large, one can use numerical compression, by sorting.

That is A[sortedIndex[i]], instead of A[array[i]]

By using same approach, one can find range with smallest sum that contains exactly N distinct elements from set S.

=== Fast mean square error calculation ===
From mean square error formula, one can observe that:

<math display="block">
\begin{align}
  &\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i = 1}^{n}\big(x_i-\bar{x}\big)^2 =\frac{1}{n}(\sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_i^2 - 2\bar{x}\sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_i + \bar{x}^2)\\
\end{align}
</math>

This means that if one have two fenwick trees; one for 
<math >x_i</math> and one for <math >x_i^2 </math>, one can in a fast way update
both of these terms in a fast way. Average <math >\bar{x} </math> can be found through
range sum query, where one divide answer with number of elements. One find the sum of <math >x_i </math>, and average <math >\bar{x} </math> at same time.
After finding <math >x_i </math>, one can just multiply it with <math >-2\bar{x} </math> get last term. Sum of <math >x_i^2 </math> is found by range summing the same elements, in the other fenwick tree.

Add all terms together, and divide with number of elements to get final answer.

By doing this one can update in O(log N) and calculate sub-ranges in O(log N)

=== Find smallest range that contains all elements in set S ===

Main idea:

Remember last index of each element in the sequence array, that is found in set S.
One have a range where both left and right side can be moved. Because only last occurence is remembered,
removing elements from left side wont do any damage. 

By setting values of last occurence of element in set S to 1, value not in set S to 0, and elment in S that is not last occurence to 0, one 
can use fenwick tree combined with range sum to find smallest range that contains all elements in set S.

<syntaxhighlight lang="c++">
for all elements in array:

if array[i] is not in S:
  update(fenw[i],0)

elif A[array[i]] is not 0:
  #Make sure to remove previous occurence of array[i] from range sum, by updating value on this index to 0.
  #Remember index of last occurence of element array[i], that is in set S
  update(fenw[A[array[i]]],0)
  update(fenw[i],1)
  A[array[i]] = i

else:
  update(fenw[i],1)
  A[array[i]] = i

calculate range sum of 0 and 1

if sum is equal to number of elements in S:
   length = i - left index
   if length < smallest length:
     smallest length = length
     leftIndexSol = left index
     rightIndexSol = i
   #Optmization, to avoid doing to many range sums.
   while left index isn't a last occurence of a number in set S:
     left index = left index + 1
i=i+1
</syntaxhighlight>


If max element is to large, one can use numerical compression, by sorting.

That is A[sortedIndex[i]], instead of A[array[i]]

By using same approach, one can find smallest range that contains exactly N distinct elements from set S.

=== Use of fenwick in k-mean algorithm, to make mean calculation faster ===

Create a sepreate fenwick tree for x and y coordinate.
Allocate empty space for coordinates to a variable N (max number of coordinates for each component) for each M components. Each of the empty spaces has value 0.

Create a list of initial means, and allocate coordinates to nearest mean (update empty allocated space, corresponding to component associated with mean, in fenwick tree).
Remember to keep track of number of coordinates in each component.

After this is done, find sum of each component and divide by number of coordinates in corresponding component.

Then swap each coordinate between components if needed, by substracting and adding values.
Remember to update number of coordinates in each components

Notice, that comparing to mean step is still O(n). The swap is slower; O(log n) compared to O(1). Mean calculation is faster; O(Mlog N), where M is number of components, compared to O(N).

=== Flow calculations, where each components has max 8 neighbours ===

Create a 2D grid, where one keep track of input flow (negative value) and output flow (positive value) in following way:

 Out  0  Out   0  Out 
 0    In  In   In  0  
 Out  In   0   In Out 
 0    In  In   In  0  
 Out  0  Out   0  Out 

By placing adjacent components next to eachother, one can create a gigantic map where one keep track of flows between components.

Here on can use 2D fenwick to sum flow across a set of components, that corresponds to a path.

In some cases, in for examples pipe systems, number of components may be many thousands.

By placing all those thousand components (for example pipes) next to eachother, one can easily calculate netto flow in a very fast way, at the same time
one can update input and output flow for each component in a fast way.

In some cases, one may have to do multiple calculations and then sum total flow of all calculations - because one only can sum in one direction at a time, due to direction change.

It could also be a crowd sensing system, where one receive GPS coordinates that is associated with a square in a map. 
By updating input and output based on square one leaved and square one entered, one can have a gigantic map of many thousands squares where
netto flow can be calculated in a fast way.

=== Data aggregation in fog computing ===

Assume one have 1000 clients A1-A1000 , a fog computer B, and a server C.

By using B as a middle man, one can create a summary of statistic that is relevant to C; based on relevant actions (max,min,product, sum) and ranges.

Communication beteen A1-A1000 and B:
#  A sends (index, value, datatype, action (add/sub/prod/max/min)) to B.
#  B checks the action, based on add or max. If max, use fenwick tree nr 1. Else if add, use fenwick tree nr 2.
#  Update array[index] with corresponding action and value.

Communication between B and C:

# B asks for a summary of statistics in format (index range, action, return data type).
# B checks the action, based on add or max. If max, use fenwick tree nr 1. Else if add, use fenwick tree nr 2.
# Use fenwick range operation corresponding to index range and action, and convert to desired data type.

=== Keep track of locations of cars in a large map ===


By letting a bit position in a number correspond to a car, one can decide that 1 means that the car is here and 0 means it doesn't.
By adding and substracting a number where it is 1 in this position and 0 in the rest, one can add and remove a car from a location.

A map can be thought of as a grid, and therfore one can use 2D fenwick tree.

Because a number only have a maximum of 64 bits, one need a new fenwick tree for each chunk of 64 cars.

Each car is tagged with (fenwickTreeNr, bitPos), and each location is tagged with an index.

If a client knows its (fenwickTreeNr,bitPos) and know what location corresponds to what index, one can just update locally
and send the index instead of the GPS coordinate.

By doing this, the server does not really know where the car is - but it can search for it in a fast way by combinding binary search (adjust right side first, and then left) and range sum query. Its
also a more secure way of dealing with locations, compared to GPS coordinates.

=== Fast Sub range integration, where values are time dependent ===

Recall simpsons formula:

<math display="block">
\begin{align}
  \int_a^b f(x)\, dx
  &\approx \frac{1}{3} h\sum_{i = 1}^{n/2}\big[f(x_{2i - 2}) + 4f(x_{2i - 1}) + f(x_{2i})\big]\\
  &= \frac{1}{3} h\big[f(x_0) + 4f(x_1) + 2f(x_2) + 4f(x_3) + 2f(x_4) + \dots + 2f(x_{n - 2}) + 4f(x_{n - 1}) + f(x_n)\big]\\
  &= \frac{1}{3} h\left[f(x_0) + 4\sum_{i = 1}^{n/2} f(x_{2i - 1}) + 2\sum_{i = 1}^{n/2 - 1} f(x_{2i}) + f(x_n)\right].
\end{align}
</math>

By adding and substracting 
<math >
\begin{align}f(x_{index}), 2f(x_{index})\end{align}
</math>, or 
<math >
\begin{align}
 4f(x_{index})
\end{align}
</math>, depending on index, to
array[index]; one can in a fast way recalculate the integral of sub ranges, when some values changes.
Notice that one can add new values by changing value of array[max index] and array[max index - 1] , and by adding new value to array[max index+1].

=== Grid surface, and collions with small objects ===

A grid surface contains many squares or faces. By making a 2D fenwick tree, one can keep track of current preasure
each square. 

By having a rule engine, that says: 
#Area >= 1000, must have p < 0.5.    action if fail: rotation of relevant squares
#Area >= 100, must have p <= 0.1,    action if fail: animation played for relevant squares
#Area >= 10, must have p <= 0.001    action if fail: translation of relevant suqares

One can check different ranges, in a fast way, to see if preasure constraint is OK.
If a constraint fail, an associated action can be used (f.ex a geometric transformation based on presure, and other parameters).

When first rule is checked for all possible ranges, one check next rule to see if a preasure constraint fail - if it does, a smaller area can be
affected by an predefined action.

Because a 2D fenwick tree is used, its possible to add and remove collisions to the grid. Such collisions could be bullets that hit a person in a 3D game, where a person
has surface grid (to simply one could have a rectangular surface grid). Rule engines controlls wether or not, one looses a finger, an arm, a head,..etc, based
on preasure distrubition). In some cases, a object may have life time - it may be removed after a fixed amount of time, or change in preasure as a function of time.

Its also handy to use 2D fenwick tree, when preasure waves is introduced (waves in water, earth quacke, explosions). Wave equations is used to predict grid squares affected, and these squares is updated accordanly. For each step (everyone is updated by a time step), one take a look on rule engine to see if some actions is triggered.

